According to the Kubernetes v1.25 release announcement, PodSecurityPolicy was removed instead of fixed because the changes needed to make it more usable would have broken existing users. It was therefore removed in favor of a friendlier replacement, Pod Security Admission, which became Stable in v1.25. PSP was first deprecated in v1.21 and removed in v1.25.

The replacement was built on a deliberately narrower scope. A rich ecosystem of external admission controllers already existed, so PSP's successor didn't need to cover every case. Its main advantage as a built-in admission controller is that it is simple to deploy and adopt. It was designed to:
- be as simple as practically possible while still flexible enough for production at scale;
- support soft rollout, so it can be added to existing clusters;
- be deactivatable partially or entirely, so it can coexist with external admission controllers for advanced use cases.

Pod Security Admission enforces the standards at the namespace level, which makes basic pod security easier to enforce without deep security knowledge. More sophisticated needs may call for a third-party solution alongside it. All of this comes from blog posts (the lowest authority tier).
